    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d160">

                <pb facs="0360" n="326" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v30/pg326"/>

                <head>160. Memorandum From the Executive Secretary of the National Security Council
                        (Smith) to President
                        Johnson<anchor n="1" xml:id="d160-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1966-06-29T19:00:00-04:00">June 29, 1966, 7 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Mr. President:</p>
                <p>Consul General Rice in Hong Kong sends in
                    a highly interesting summary of what is apparently going on in Communist
                    China.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">Bromley Smith</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d160-a1.xml" xpointer="d160-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d160-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d160-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Country File, China, Vol. VI. No
                    classification marking. A handwritten “L” on
                        Smith’s note indicates that it was
                    seen by the President. The attachment is a retyped copy of telegram 2327 from
                    Hong Kong, June 25, and is identical in substance to the telegram as received, a
                    copy of which is ibid. Another copy is in Department of State, Central Files,
                        POL
                    CHICOM.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
